## Before the first run

Setup writes placeholders where it cannot know your workspace. Replace them and
check that the data exists before you deploy:

| Where | What to set |
| --- | --- |
| `deployment/targets.yml` | `host` under `test`{{if eq .personal_development_targets "true"}} and `test_development`{{end}}: replace `https://replace-test.example.invalid` with your workspace URL. |
| `deployment/variables.yml` | `test_catalog`, `test_input_schema`, `test_output_schema` and `test_metadata_schema` (`REPLACE_TEST_*`){{if eq .personal_development_targets "true"}}, plus `test_development_input_schema`, `test_development_output_schema` and `test_development_metadata_schema`{{end}}. |
| Commands below | `YOUR_PROFILE`: an authenticated Databricks CLI profile for that workspace. |
| Unity Catalog grants | The job identity reads the inputs, writes the output schema and registers models in the metadata schema; see [required access](https://www.skyulf.com/manual/user_guide/databricks_deployment.html#data-model-and-experiment-access). |
| Unity Catalog | The catalog, schemas and source table must exist. The table needs the record key, input and target columns from setup{{if ne .prediction_delivery "endpoint"}}, and Change Data Feed on before the first score{{end}}. |
| `config/training.yml` | A numeric `quality_threshold`{{if eq .training_layout "multi_target"}} in every `models` entry{{end}}, if you will approve the first champion or promote automatically. Set it before training; a trained version keeps its policy. |
| `deployment/variables.yml` and `deployment/targets.yml` (optional) | Monitoring runs only when `monitoring_enabled` is `"true"`. It writes to `monitoring_catalog`.`monitoring_schema` (variables.yml); the catalog defaults to each target's own catalog and the schema to `skyulf_monitoring`. Use the same schema in every project so all models appear in one dashboard. `deployment/targets.yml` holds each target's dashboard and the development targets' monitoring settings; only one project creates a dashboard, the others connect to it there. |

For the shared `test` target, check that `test_root_path` in `deployment/variables.yml`
is a workspace folder your team can write to. The local checks below do not flag
these placeholders.

Source arrangement: **{{.source_layout}}**. Initialization does not create source
tables. For existing tables, use the [source checks](GUIDE.md#source-data) after
installing dependencies in step 1. Each record key must identify one observation;
keep the actual target out of model inputs. Use the same guide if tables do not
exist yet or outcomes arrive separately.

{{if ne .prediction_delivery "endpoint"}}Before training, choose `initial_scoring` in `config/inference.yml`:
`full_snapshot` includes existing rows; `from_now` records a zero-row checkpoint
and scores later inserts. Training may automatically start scoring, and this
choice cannot change on an existing prediction target.

{{end}}## First train and {{if eq .prediction_delivery "endpoint"}}serve{{else}}score{{end}}

Replace `YOUR_PROFILE` in every command.

1. **Check locally.** Smoke and preview read configuration only, not source data
   or workspace access:

   ```powershell
   uv pip install -r deployment/train-requirements.txt
   python src/tools/smoke.py
   python src/tools/preview.py --action train
   ```

2. **Validate and deploy:**

   ```powershell
   databricks bundle validate --strict -t {{if eq .personal_development_targets "true"}}test_development{{else}}test{{end}} --profile YOUR_PROFILE
   databricks bundle deploy -t {{if eq .personal_development_targets "true"}}test_development{{else}}test{{end}} --profile YOUR_PROFILE
   ```

3. **Train:**

   ```powershell
   databricks bundle run -t {{if eq .personal_development_targets "true"}}test_development{{else}}test{{end}} --profile YOUR_PROFILE train
   ```

4. **Read the report.** {{if eq .training_layout "multi_target"}}Open `train_<name>`, `training_report` (`models_report.py`) and `model_decision`;
   review every component before activating the set.
{{else if eq .training_layout "model_competition"}}Open the candidate fits, `select_best_model` and `training_report`. Only the
   winner is compared on the protected holdout; a failed winner does not promote a runner-up.
{{else}}Open `train_and_tune` and `training_report`. Registration alone does not
   activate a candidate.
{{end}}   Start with **Where to look** in `training_report` > Output.

{{$pinned := and (eq .prediction_delivery "batch") (ne .training_layout "multi_target") (eq .score_model_selection "pinned_version") (ne .promotion_policy "automatic")}}{{if $pinned}}5. **Score the new version.** `training_report` > **Where to look** names the
   registered version as `<model> vN`. Score that version for this run only:

   ```powershell
   databricks bundle run -t {{if eq .personal_development_targets "true"}}test_development{{else}}test{{end}} --profile YOUR_PROFILE score --params score_model_version=N
   ```

   Replace `N` with that version. This needs no approval and changes neither
   `config/inference.yml` nor any alias. Scheduled runs use `model_version` in
   `config/inference.yml`; set it to the same version when you are ready.
   Check the committed output{{if eq .monitoring "true"}}, then `monitoring_report` and `evaluate_retraining`.
   Before monitoring an unactivated pinned candidate for the first time, prepare its
   [reference](https://www.skyulf.com/manual/user_guide/databricks_monitoring_enrollment.html){{end}}.

6. **Approve later (optional).** Approval makes a version the champion, which
   champion scoring needs. It uses the `quality_threshold` set before training. Copy the
   **Available action: approve** block from the report (the first approval uses expected
   champion `none`) into the train job's **Run now with different parameters**; see
   [operator actions](https://www.skyulf.com/manual/user_guide/databricks_lifecycle.html#operator-actions-through-run-now).
{{if eq .score_follow_champion "true"}}   An approval starts a score run by itself; inspect that run instead of starting another.
{{end}}
{{else}}5. **Approve.** {{if eq .promotion_policy "automatic"}}This project promotes automatically when the quality gates pass; the
   report shows the decision. To roll back, copy the **If rollback is needed** block
{{else}}Copy the **Available action: approve** block from the report (the first
   approval uses expected champion `none`)
{{end}}   into the train job's **Run now with different parameters**; see
   [operator actions](https://www.skyulf.com/manual/user_guide/databricks_lifecycle.html#operator-actions-through-run-now).

{{if ne .prediction_delivery "endpoint"}}6. **Score:**

   ```powershell
   databricks bundle run -t {{if eq .personal_development_targets "true"}}test_development{{else}}test{{end}} --profile YOUR_PROFILE score
   ```

   {{if eq .training_layout "multi_target"}}Ordinary runs score the approved set champion; `score_model_version` selects
   a concrete set version for one run.
{{else if eq .score_model_selection "champion"}}Scoring resolves the champion alias, so a version must be promoted first.
{{else}}Scoring uses the pinned `model_version` in `config/inference.yml` (`"1"` for
   a new model) and needs no approval. Change it to score a later version.
{{end}}{{if eq .score_follow_champion "true"}}   An approval or promotion starts a score run by itself; inspect that run first.
{{end}}   Check the committed output{{if eq .monitoring "true"}}, then `monitoring_report` and `evaluate_retraining`.
   Before monitoring an unactivated pinned candidate for the first time, prepare its
   [reference](https://www.skyulf.com/manual/user_guide/databricks_monitoring_enrollment.html){{end}}.

{{end}}{{end}}{{if ne .prediction_delivery "batch"}}
## Serve the model

1. **Publish a version.** Without parameters the job serves the champion. To try a
   registered version before approving it, name it for this run:

   ```powershell
   databricks bundle run -t {{if eq .personal_development_targets "true"}}test_development{{else}}test{{end}} --profile YOUR_PROFILE serving_endpoint --params model_version=N
   ```

   Personal development targets always run it manually. In production Bundle mode,
   `serving_endpoint.follow_champion: true` submits this run after a champion
   change; training success only confirms that submission, so open that child run.
2. **Check what is live.** In the `serving_endpoint` run output, `model_uri` is the
   version the endpoint now serves and `verification` counts the sample rows whose
   REST (`rest_compared`) and SQL (`sql_compared`) predictions matched the saved model.
   `status: READY` alone does not prove the new version is serving.
3. **Call it.** Copy `examples` from the same output: `rest_path` with `rest_body`,
   the `sql` query and the `python` SDK call. Callers need `CAN_QUERY` on the
   endpoint and `EXECUTE` on the SQL function: add them to `permissions` (and
   `function_permissions` if they differ) in `config/serving.yml`.
4. **Monitor requests.** With target `monitoring_enabled: "true"`, payload logging and a
   monitoring store, successful verification enrolls the endpoint. Run the monitoring
   job after real requests arrive; no batch score run is needed.

See [serving requests](GUIDE.md#serving-requests) for costs, logging choices and rollout.

{{end}}## Learn by making one change

After the first successful run, change one setting and compare two fits of the same data:

1. **Record the first fit.** In `training_report` > **Where to look**, note the
   version (`<model> vN`), the holdout metric and the **Training data** column's
   `Delta version`.
2. **Fix the data.** In `config/training.yml` defaults, set `training_table_version`
   to that Delta version, so the second fit reads the same rows. The split seed
   (`random_state`) already stays the same.
3. **Change one setting.** For a tuned model, raise the search size under the
   model's `tuning`, for example:

   ```yaml
   # before
       tuning:
         n_trials: 3
   # after
       tuning:
         n_trials: 10
   ```

   For a fixed model, change one value under its `model` > `params` instead.
   `python src/tools/preview.py --action train` shows the new value before you deploy.
4. **Deploy and train again.** A new version `vN+1` is registered; `vN` and its
   files do not change.
5. **Compare.** Put both training reports side by side: same source version and
   holdout rows, so a metric difference comes from your change. More trials do
   not guarantee a better score; keep whichever version is better for your goal.
{{if ne .prediction_delivery "endpoint"}}   Score the new version for one run with `--params score_model_version=N+1` and
   compare one record's prediction from both versions.
{{end}}6. If monitoring is enabled, distinguish drift, label coverage and measured
   performance using [monitoring](GUIDE.md#monitoring).

